    "ExplanationCorrect": "The debt-to-equity ratio of 2.8× and operating cash flow to total debt of 0.12 warrant the greatest weight. Under ASC 205-40 and fundamental credit analysis principles, an integrated ratio assessment requires evaluating both liquidity and solvency, but solvency risk must be prioritized when an industry downturn is projected because insolvency is terminal while liquidity pressure is transitional. Three factors make Meridian's apparent liquidity strength misleading: (1) $28 million in inventory represents 60% of current assets and may become impaired or slow-moving in a retail contraction; (2) operating cash flow to total debt of 0.12 means it would take over eight years of current operating cash flow to repay total debt, leaving no margin for the projected revenue decline; and (3) the debt-to-equity ratio of 2.8× exceeds the industry median of 1.1× by 155%, indicating Meridian is structurally over-leveraged relative to competitors. Professional judgment requires weighing solvency indicators more heavily than liquidity indicators when the economic environment is deteriorating.",
    "ExplanationWrongA": "",
    "ExplanationWrongB": "Prioritizing liquidity ratios while disregarding the 2.8× debt-to-equity ratio and 0.12 operating cash flow coverage is inconsistent with fundamental credit analysis. For a retailer facing a projected 4–6% industry contraction, strong current and quick ratios — which depend heavily on inventory values that become uncertain in a downturn — do not protect against solvency risk. High leverage with thin cash flow coverage threatens the entity's ability to meet obligations even when working capital appears ample. A candidate selecting this option commits the error of treating ratio analysis as a single-dimension exercise.",
    "ExplanationWrongC": "Focusing exclusively on times interest earned of 1.9× ignores the interdependence of financial ratios in credit analysis. While a low TIE ratio is concerning, it cannot be assessed in isolation — the debt-to-equity ratio, operating cash flow coverage, and liquidity position all inform whether the company can sustain or improve its debt service capacity. No authoritative standard establishes 2.0× as a bright-line threshold for imminent default.",
    "ExplanationWrongD": "Inventory turnover of 4.2× does not offset the structural problems created by a 2.8× debt-to-equity ratio and declining operating cash flow. Efficient inventory management improves working capital efficiency but does not directly generate cash for debt service — inventory must first be sold, and in a projected retail downturn, both the volume and margin on those sales are under pressure. A borrowing base tied to inventory would amplify risk because collateral value declines precisely when the borrower most needs liquidity.",
