    "CorrectChoice": "D",
    "ExplanationCorrect": "Under ASC 350 goodwill impairment is evaluated at the reporting-unit level; if carrying exceeds fair value, the impairment equals the excess limited to the goodwill carrying amount, measured as the difference between the fair value of the reporting unit and the fair value of its identifiable net assets (implied goodwill). Step: carrying 14,200,000 exceeds fair value 11,500,000 by 2,700,000, so the test fails. Implied goodwill = fair value of unit 11,500,000 - fair value of identifiable net assets 9,100,000 = 2,400,000. Carrying goodwill 4,800,000 exceeds implied 2,400,000 by 2,400,000, so impairment is 2,400,000 (not 2,700,000 which would impair identifiable assets too). New goodwill = 2,400,000. Recomputed independently: 14,200,000 - 11,500,000 = 2,700,000 shortfall; 11,500,000 - 9,100,000 = 2,400,000 implied; 4,800,000 - 2,400,000 = 2,400,000 impairment. Business interpretation: M&A director Raj Patel should recommend a $2,400,000 charge to operating income, disclose the Level 3 inputs, and advise the committee that only goodwill is impaired, leaving other assets at their carrying values. The trap is impairing the full shortfall or all goodwill.",
