    "ExplanationCorrect": "Evaluating the swap requires tracing the effect of higher interest and a smaller share base through the GAAP-based income statement (ASC 220; interest per ASC 470). Recomputed: Before — EBT = $1,200,000 - $150,000 = $1,050,000; tax at 30% = $315,000; net income = $735,000; EPS = $735,000 / 200,000 shares = $3.68. After — EBT = $1,200,000 - $250,000 = $950,000; tax = $285,000; net income = $665,000; shares = 150,000; EPS = $665,000 / 150,000 = $4.43. EPS rises roughly 21 percent, but the degree of financial leverage climbs from 1.14 to 1.26, so a 10 percent drop in EBIT now cuts EPS by about 12.6 percent instead of 11.4 percent. Because the higher EPS is contingent on EBIT holding at $1,200,000, Adaeze Onuorah should approve the swap only if Flash's operating earnings are stable enough to make the added interest risk acceptable.",
    "ExplanationWrongA": "Although the EPS figures ($3.68 to $4.43) are computed correctly, the conclusion ignores the cost of added risk; the higher interest burden raises Flash's degree of financial leverage, so the EPS gains reverse quickly if EBIT falls — declaring the swap clearly superior at every operating level ignores the downside that leverage creates.",
    "ExplanationWrongB": "This choice assumes the higher interest charge and the share reduction exactly cancel, leaving EPS unchanged; the arithmetic does not support that — interest rises $100,000, reducing after-tax earnings by $70,000, while the share base shrinks 25 percent, and the combined effect lifts EPS from $3.68 to $4.43 instead of holding it flat.",
    "ExplanationWrongC": "This choice reports EPS falling to $3.33, an error caused by dividing after-tax income of $665,000 by the original 200,000 shares; the $1,000,000 equity repurchase reduces the share count to 150,000, and dividing by that reduced count shows EPS actually rises to $4.43 — the repurchase concentrates the same earnings on fewer shares rather than diluting them.",
