    "ExplanationWrongA": "This choice mischaracterizes financial leverage. Interest is fixed, but that is the source of amplification, not stability: after the fixed interest claim is paid, any change in EBIT falls entirely on the residual earnings available to shareholders, so EPS swings by a larger percentage than EBIT. A fixed charge increases, not decreases, the volatility of the residual that common shareholders receive.",
    "ExplanationWrongB": "This choice confuses the direction of operating leverage. Fixed costs do not vary with volume, which is precisely why operating income swings MORE than sales: a decline in sales still must absorb the same fixed cost base, so EBIT falls proportionally more. Fixed costs do not cushion swings; they amplify them by creating an earnings base that shrinks faster than revenue.",
