    "UniqueConceptKey": "A-424-liquidity-policy-recommendation",
    "Stem": "Maya Caldwell, Flash Manufacturing's treasurer, must recommend a working capital policy for the coming year. Flash faces highly volatile customer orders: some months cash inflows are 40% below plan, and the company has no committed revolving credit line. Its return on assets is currently below the industry average. Which policy best balances Flash's liquidity risk and profitability objectives?",
    "Choices": {
      "A": "Aggressive policy — minimize current-asset balances and maximize short-term debt to lift return on assets.",
      "B": "Conservative policy — hold large cash and marketable-security buffers financed with long-term debt regardless of cost.",
      "C": "Moderate policy — hold current assets that cover predictable peaks in cash demand, with short-term borrowing reserved for unexpected spikes.",
      "D": "Aggressive policy with maximum reliance on accounts-payable deferral to fund working capital."
    },
    "CorrectChoice": "C",
    "ExplanationCorrect": "Flash's situation has two defining features: volatile cash inflows and no committed credit line. An aggressive policy (A) leaves no buffer for the 40% demand shortfalls Flash actually experiences, and without a committed revolver the company cannot bridge a cash shortfall quickly without distress sales of assets. A conservative policy (B) eliminates liquidity risk but drags return on assets further below industry average by parking capital in low-yielding liquid assets financed at long-term rates — an unacceptable cost given Flash's profitability gap. The moderate policy (C) matches the current-asset base to the predictable peak of cash demand so normal fluctuations are absorbed internally, while reserving short-term borrowing for genuinely unexpected spikes; this hedges the volatility risk that threatens Flash most while limiting the profitability drag to the buffer actually needed. Reliance on payable deferral alone (D) is not a policy but a credit gamble: stretching payables strains supplier relationships and concentrates liquidity risk in a single, fragile source.",
