    "CorrectChoice": "B",
    "ExplanationCorrect": "Inventory turnover measures how many times a firm sells and replaces its inventory during the period. The standard convention uses average inventory to smooth seasonal and purchasing fluctuations, computed as (beginning + ending) / 2 = ($1,200,000 + $1,800,000) / 2 = $1,500,000. Inventory turnover = COGS / average inventory = $6,000,000 / $1,500,000 = 4.00 times. Recomputed independently: (1,200,000 + 1,800,000) / 2 = 1,500,000; 6,000,000 / 1,500,000 = 4.00. Using average inventory is the IMA CMA standard because turnover ratios pair a flow measure (COGS) with a stock measure (inventory), and averaging mitigates the distortion from period-end spikes or troughs in inventory levels.",
