    "ExplanationCorrect": "A common-size income statement expresses every line item as a percentage of net sales, enabling comparison across firms of different sizes. Net profit margin = net income / net sales = $900,000 / $10,000,000 = 9.0%. Recomputed independently: 900,000 / 10,000,000 = 0.09 = 9%. Every line is scaled to the same sales base: COGS is 65.0%, operating expenses are 20.0%, and net income is 9.0%, which together reconcile: 100% − 65% − 20% = 15% operating income, less interest and taxes to 9% net. The common-size approach removes size effects so Flash can be compared directly to industry peers on profitability structure.",
    "ExplanationWrongA": "",
    "ExplanationWrongB": "Choice B (15.0%) is the operating margin, computed as gross profit minus operating expenses over sales: ($10,000,000 − $6,500,000 − $2,000,000) / $10,000,000 = $1,500,000 / $10,000,000 = 15%. Operating margin excludes interest and taxes; net profit margin is the bottom-line percentage of sales after all expenses. The two margins answer different questions.",
    "ExplanationWrongC": "Choice C (13.8%) arises from dividing net income by cost of goods sold ($900,000 / $6,500,000 = 13.8%) instead of by net sales. Common-size income statements use sales as the 100% base for every line; using COGS as the base distorts the comparison and produces a figure with no standard interpretation.",
    "ExplanationWrongD": "Choice D (35.0%) is the gross profit margin: ($10,000,000 − $6,500,000) / $10,000,000 = 35%. Gross margin measures the markup over cost of goods sold before operating expenses; it is not the net profit margin, which reflects the full cost structure down to net income.",
